.SH CONFIGURATION
JarvisAgent reads
.B config.json
from the current working directory at startup.
If the file is missing, the application prints an error and exits.
.PP
The following fields are recognized:
.TP
.B "file format identifier"
(number) Internal format version for the config file.
.TP
.B "description"
(string) A human\-readable description of this configuration.
.TP
.B "author"
(string) Author or owner of this configuration.
.TP
.B "queue folder"
(string, \fBrequired\fR) Relative or absolute path to the queue directory.
JarvisAgent monitors this folder for incoming files that trigger AI requests.
.TP
.B "workflows folder"
(string) Relative or absolute path to the directory containing
.B .jcwf
workflow definition files.
Defaults to
.IR workflows/ .
.TP
.B "port"
(number) Web server listen port.
.I 0
= auto\-select (8080 for HTTP, 8443 for HTTPS).
Valid range: 1\(en65535.
Default: 0.
.TP
.B "max threads"
(number) Worker\-thread pool size.
Default: 16.
Valid range: 1\(en256.
.TP
.B "max inflight ai calls"
(number) Maximum concurrent AI requests dispatched via HTTP/2.
Decoupled from thread pool size since requests are multiplexed on a single
I/O thread.
Default: 100.
Valid range: 1\(en1000.
.TP
.B "python engines"
(number) Number of Python sub\-interpreters (each with its own GIL) for
parallel Python task execution.
Requires Python 3.12+.
Default: 4.
Valid range: 1\(en16.
.TP
.B "engine sleep time in run loop in ms"
(number) Main loop sleep duration in milliseconds.
Controls CPU usage vs. responsiveness.
Default: 10.
Valid range: 1\(en256.
.TP
.B "max file size in kB"
(number) Maximum input file size in kilobytes.
Files larger than this are chunked before being sent to the AI.
Default: 20.
Valid range: 1\(en256.
.TP
.B "jcwf batch size"
(number) Number of tasks per batch when the AI JCWF generator fans out to
multiple parallel calls for large workflows.
Workflows with fewer tasks than this threshold are generated in a single call.
Default: 10.
.TP
.B "verbose"
(boolean) Enable verbose logging output.
.TP
.B "keys_file"
(string) Path to the encrypted API keys file.
Default:
.IR keys.json.enc .
See
.B ENVIRONMENT
below.
.TP
.B "use_bash"
(boolean) Windows only: prefer bash (MSYS2/Git Bash) over PowerShell for
shell tasks.
Default: false.
Ignored on Linux/macOS.
.TP
.B "TlsCert"
(string) Path to a PEM\-format TLS certificate file.
Both \fBTlsCert\fR and \fBTlsKey\fR must be set to enable HTTPS.
See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B "TlsKey"
(string) Path to a PEM\-format TLS private key file.
.TP
.B "TrustedProxyHeader"
(string) HTTP header name for gateway\-injected user identity
(e.g. \fIX\-Forwarded\-User\fR).
Engine edition only.
See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B "TrustedRoleHeader"
(string) HTTP header name for gateway\-injected user role
(e.g. \fIX\-Forwarded\-Role\fR).
Engine edition only.
.TP
.B "MaxRequestBodyMB"
(number) Maximum HTTP request body size in megabytes.
Oversized requests are rejected with HTTP 413 before parsing.
Engine edition only.
Default: 10.
.TP
.B "API index"
(number) Zero\-based index of the default AI interface to use from the
.B "API interfaces"
array.
.TP
.B "API interfaces"
(array) List of AI provider configurations.
Each entry is an object with the following fields:
.RS
.TP
.B url
(string, \fBrequired\fR) The API endpoint URL
(e.g. \fIhttps://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions\fR).
.TP
.B model
(string) The model name (e.g. \fIgpt\-4o\fR, \fIgemini\-2.5\-flash\fR).
.TP
.B API
(string) The reply parser type.
\fBAPI1\fR \(em OpenAI\-compatible chat completions (OpenAI, Google Gemini via
OpenAI\-compat endpoint, Ollama, any /v1/chat/completions provider).
\fBAPI2\fR \(em OpenAI Responses API (GPT\-5 and later models).
\fBAPI3\fR \(em Google Gemini native API (uses \fIx\-goog\-api\-key\fR header
and \fI/models/{model}:generateContent\fR URL scheme).
.TP
.B name
(string) Human\-readable name for this interface.
Auto\-generated from URL domain + model if omitted.
.TP
.B description
(string) Optional description of this interface.
.TP
.B key_name
(string) Name of the API key provider to use from the encrypted keys file
(e.g. "openai", "google", "anthropic").
.RE
.
.SS Rate\-limit configuration
.PP
Every interface has an associated adaptive controller that decides how
aggressively to dispatch requests, plus a size\-aware in\-flight budget
that bounds each request's curl timeout.  Both are tuned by the optional
\fBrate_limit\fR block on the interface:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B {
.B "    \(dqAPI\(dq: \(dqAPI4\(dq,"
.B "    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttps://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages\(dq,"
.B "    \(dqmodel\(dq: \(dqclaude-sonnet-4-6\(dq,"
.B "    \(dqkey_name\(dq: \(dqAnthropic\(dq,"
.B "    \(dqrate_limit\(dq: {"
.B "        \(dqinitial_concurrency_probe\(dq: 4,"
.B "        \(dqmax_concurrency\(dq: 48,"
.B "        \(dqmax_retries_429\(dq: 10,"
.B "        \(dqmax_retries_transient\(dq: 2,"
.B "        \(dqbase_retry_ms\(dq: 1000,"
.B "        \(dqrequest_budget\(dq: {"
.B "            \(dqper_1k_input_token_seconds\(dq: 0.5,"
.B "            \(dqper_1k_output_token_seconds\(dq: 5.0,"
.B "            \(dqfixed_overhead_seconds\(dq: 5.0,"
.B "            \(dqsafety_margin_factor\(dq: 4.0,"
.B "            \(dqmin_seconds\(dq: 60.0,"
.B "            \(dqmax_seconds\(dq: 600.0"
.B "        }"
.B "    }"
.B }
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBConcurrency / retry knobs\fR:
.RS
.TP
.B initial_concurrency_probe
Starting AIMD cap before the controller has observed any response.
Default per InterfaceType: Anthropic 4, OpenAI 8, Empty 4.
Set to 1 for a Tier\-1 Anthropic account; raise to 16+ for Tier\-3+.
.TP
.B max_concurrency
Hard ceiling AIMD growth never crosses, regardless of how many clean
completions accumulate.
Default 48 (the HTTP/2 stream cap).
Set lower to pace burn rate on cost\-capped accounts \(em this is the only
cost\-shaping knob in 1.0.
.TP
.B max_retries_429
Number of attempts after a 429 before giving up.
Default 10 (controller's predictive gating means real 429s are rare in
practice).
.TP
.B max_retries_transient
Attempts after a transient HTTP error (400/500/502/503).
Default 2.
.TP
.B base_retry_ms
First retry delay; subsequent retries use exponential backoff
\fBbase * 2^n\fR.
Default 1000.
.RE
.PP
\fBSize\-aware request budget\fR \(em every request gets a curl timeout
(\fBCURLOPT_TIMEOUT_MS\fR) computed from:
.PP
.RS
.nf
seconds = (input_tokens / 1000 \(mu per_1k_input_token_seconds)
        + (max_output_tokens / 1000 \(mu per_1k_output_token_seconds)
        + fixed_overhead_seconds
seconds *= safety_margin_factor
seconds  = clamp(seconds, min_seconds, max_seconds)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Curl's timeout only counts time \fIon the wire\fR \(em inbox waits,
controller throttling, and retry\-queue backoffs don't burn the budget.
Each retry creates a fresh easy handle with a fresh budget.
.PP
\fBTuning examples:\fR
.PP
\fITier\-1 Anthropic (free / starter, ~5 RPM Sonnet):\fR
.RS
.nf
\(dqrate_limit\(dq: { \(dqinitial_concurrency_probe\(dq: 1, \(dqmax_concurrency\(dq: 4 }
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fITier\-3 Anthropic (production, ~50 RPM Sonnet):\fR
.RS
.nf
\(dqrate_limit\(dq: { \(dqinitial_concurrency_probe\(dq: 8, \(dqmax_concurrency\(dq: 32 }
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fICost\-capped account \(em pace burn at ~10 in\-flight max regardless of provider tier:\fR
.RS
.nf
\(dqrate_limit\(dq: { \(dqmax_concurrency\(dq: 10 }
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fIWorkflow expects very long Opus responses (8K\-12K tokens):\fR
.RS
.nf
\(dqrate_limit\(dq: {
    \(dqrequest_budget\(dq: {
        \(dqper_1k_output_token_seconds\(dq: 15.0,
        \(dqmin_seconds\(dq: 120.0,
        \(dqmax_seconds\(dq: 1200.0
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBVerifying tuning\fR \(em \fBGET /api/debug/signals\fR (debug builds only)
exposes per\-(host, modelFamily) controller state at
\fBdispatcher_controllers[]\fR: current AIMD cap, streak since last 429,
last observation (remaining requests / tokens, reset times), last
consumed input/output tokens.
Use this to confirm the controller is doing what you expect before
scaling up.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
JarvisAgent reads
.B no credentials from the environment.
Provider keys come exclusively from the encrypted
.BR keys.json.enc ,
and the master password is supplied
.B only
at runtime via the dashboard login flow or
.BR "POST /api/settings/keys/unlock" .
There is no environment\-variable shortcut for either.
An env\-var key would sit in plaintext in the process environment (readable via
.IR /proc/PID/environ ,
.I "docker inspect" ,
or a crash dump) \(em exactly the unsecured exposure the encrypted keystore
exists to prevent.
The master password is held exclusively in
.BR mlock() \-protected
memory
.RB ( SecureString )
and never lands in process listings or core dumps.
.
.SH FIRST STEPS AFTER RESTART
Every time JarvisAgent starts (cold boot, systemctl restart, container
restart), the encrypted key stores
.RB ( keys.json.enc
and
.BR mcp_keys.json.enc )
are sealed \(em the master password is held only in
.BR mlock() \-protected
memory and is never persisted. An admin must unlock them before any
credential\-dependent work can proceed.
.PP
\fBWhat does not work until you unlock:\fR
.IP \(bu 3
MCP API key authentication \(em all
.B "Authorization: Bearer mcp_..."
requests return 401 / 403 because the key hashes can't be compared.
.IP \(bu
AI provider API calls \(em workflows with
.B ai_call
tasks fail at dispatch time with a "no providers configured" error.
.IP \(bu
OAuth token refresh (OneDrive, Google Sheets, etc.) \(em access tokens
won't be re\-issued when they expire.
.IP \(bu
Cloud connections that depend on encrypted credentials (Snowflake
key\-pair, Postgres/SMTP BasicAuth, Slack bot token, etc.).
.PP
\fBWhat does work:\fR
.IP \(bu 3
\fIGET /api/status\fR \(em reports \fB"keys_unlocked": false\fR so monitors
can alert.
.IP \(bu
\fIPOST /api/auth/mcp-keys/activate\fR \(em new users can exchange an
enrollment token without the store being unlocked (the token itself is
the auth).
.IP \(bu
\fIPOST /api/settings/keys/unlock\fR \(em the endpoint that unseals
everything.
.IP \(bu
Studio browser UI on localhost (no auth required).
.PP
\fBHow to unlock:\fR
.IP 1. 4
\fBDashboard\fR: load the Engine dashboard in a browser, enter your MCP
key on the login page. If the key store is sealed, the Settings modal
prompts for the master password; provide it once and the session
proceeds normally afterwards.
.IP 2.
\fBREST\fR:
.EX
curl -sS -X POST https://host:8443/api/settings/keys/unlock \\
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
     -d '{"master_password":"..."}'
.EE
Returns 200 with
.B "mcp_keys_loaded": true
on success, 401 on wrong password.
.IP 3.
\fBCI / Docker / automation\fR: call the unlock endpoint from a startup
script using a secret you mount in (Docker secrets, AWS Secrets Manager,
HashiCorp Vault). The old
.B JARVIS_MASTER_PASSWORD
environment variable was removed on purpose \(em the password never
appears in process listings or
.BR "docker inspect" .
.PP
\fBFirst-run only:\fR on the very first start with an empty MCP key
store, JarvisAgent prints a bootstrap admin enrollment token to the
application log
.RB ( log/log.txt
plus the TUI). Activate it via
.I POST /api/auth/mcp-keys/activate
to obtain the initial admin MCP key, then unlock as above on subsequent
restarts.
.
.SH MCP API KEYS \(em ENROLLMENT, ACTIVATION, USE
Every credential in JarvisAgent is an MCP API key \(em a per\-user,
individually\-revocable bearer token. Keys are prefixed
.B mcp_
because the same credential is used by the \fBM\fRodel \fBC\fRontext
\fBP\fRrotocol sidecar (the machine path: Claude Code, CI bots, custom
agents) \fIand\fR by humans signing into the dashboard in a browser (the
user path). Each key carries an identity, role, adhoc flag, disk quota,
and retention ceiling on the server side.
.PP
\fBWhy one credential type for both paths.\fR An agent holding Alice's
key is authorised \fIexactly\fR as Alice is. If Alice can submit adhoc
workflows, so can her Claude Code instance; if Alice cannot shut down
the server, neither can her agent. Every action \(em whether it
originated from a browser click or an LLM tool call \(em traces back to
a single human user in the audit log. Agents genuinely act \fBon behalf
of\fR their user, without a separate service\-account pattern. One
issuance, one revocation, one audit trail.
.PP
Three actors participate in the flows below: the \fBadmin\fR who issues
credentials, the \fBuser\fR who activates and uses them, and the
\fBagent\fR process (Claude Code, a CI bot, a custom script) that holds
the final key on behalf of that user.
.
.SS I'm a new user \(em how do I get a key?
Your admin hands you an enrollment token (\fBenroll_...\fR) out\-of\-band
\(em Slack DM, email, in person. The token is single\-use and expires
within 30 minutes of creation (60 minutes for the first\-run bootstrap
token).
.IP 1. 4
Open the JarvisAgent dashboard (\fIhttps://<host>:8443/\fR).
.IP 2.
At the Sign\-in dialog, paste the \fBenroll_...\fR token into the input
field. The dialog auto\-switches to \fBActivate enrollment token\fR with
your token pre\-filled.
.IP 3.
Click \fBActivate\fR. Your real MCP API key appears on screen. \fBIt is
shown exactly once.\fR
.IP 4.
Tick the "I have saved this MCP API key" checkbox. The \fBSign in with
this key\fR button flips from red to green.
.IP 5.
Copy the key into your password manager (or Claude Code config), then
click \fBSign in with this key\fR to enter the dashboard.
.PP
If you lose the key there is no recovery path \(em ask the admin for a
fresh enrollment token. Don't share your key; treat it like a password.
.
.SS I'm a user \(em how do I use my key with Claude Code?
The MCP sidecar
.RB ( code/mcp/dist/index.js )
reads the key from
.B J9T_TOKEN
or from a file pointed to by
.B J9T_TOKEN_FILE
(recommended; chmod 600, gitignored).
.PP
Minimal
.B .mcp.json
snippet:
.EX
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "j9t": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/abs/path/to/jarvisAgent/mcp/dist/index.js"],
      "env": {
        "J9T_URL": "https://localhost:8443",
        "J9T_TOKEN_FILE": "/abs/path/to/.mcp_admin_token"
      }
    }
  }
}
.EE
Restart Claude Code after editing. Verify with the
.B whoami
MCP tool \(em it should return your user and role.
.
.SS I'm a user \(em my key is about to expire
MCP keys expire 90 days after activation by default. Within 30 days of
expiry, responses carry an
.B "X-Key-Expires-In: <N>d"
header. Renew yourself at
.I POST /api/auth/mcp-keys/self-renew
with your current (still\-valid) key \(em a fresh key is issued, the old
one enters a 24\-hour grace period then disables. The same role / adhoc
flag / quota carry over; self\-renewal cannot escalate privilege. If
your key has already expired, the admin must issue a new enrollment
token.
.
.SS I'm an admin \(em how do I onboard a new user?
.IP 1. 4
Sign into the dashboard with your admin MCP key.
.IP 2.
Open \fBSettings\fR (gear icon) \(rA \fBMCP Keys\fR tab.
.IP 3.
Click \fB+ Create enrollment\fR.
.IP 4.
Fill in the user's email/username, role (admin/operator/viewer), adhoc
flag, disk quota, default retention, and expiry. Click \fBCreate\fR.
.IP 5.
Copy the \fBenroll_...\fR token and share it over a secure channel. You
never see the user's real MCP key \(em by design.
.PP
To \fBrevoke\fR: Settings \(rA MCP Keys \(rA \fBRevoke\fR on the row.
Immediate; subsequent requests 401 and are audit\-logged. To
\fBre-enroll\fR (rotate key or change role/quota): issue a fresh
enrollment token; the user activates it and the previous key enters the
24\-hour grace period.
.
.SS I'm an admin \(em how do I deploy scripts?
Scripts under
.B scripts/
are the only way for adhoc workflows to execute shell / Python code \(em
agents can reference them but cannot upload code.
.IP 1. 4
Drop the file into
.B scripts/
on the j9t host. Shell scripts are
.B .sh
/
.BR .ps1 ;
Python modules are
.B .py
importable as
.BR scripts.<name> .
.IP 2.
Add a metadata header so it surfaces in the catalog:
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# @jarvis-script
# @short: One-line description
# @params: ARG1 ARG2 ARG3
# @description: Longer explanation, possibly wrapping
#   onto indented continuation lines.
# @outputs: Free-text description of produced artifacts
.EE
.IP 3.
Make it executable:
.B chmod +x scripts/my-script.sh
(shell only).
.IP 4.
In the dashboard, open \fBSettings \(rA Scripts\fR and click
\fBRefresh\fR to trigger an on\-demand rescan. No j9t restart required.
The
.B list_scripts
MCP tool and
.I GET /api/scripts
endpoint now surface the new script; agents can reference it in adhoc
submissions.
.PP
Scripts without the
.B @jarvis-script
marker still appear in the catalog but are flagged \(em useful for
internal helper modules
.RB ( scripts/helpers/*.py )
that agents shouldn't reference directly as task entries.
.
.SS I'm an admin \(em how do agents use adhoc workflows?
An authorised agent (MCP key with
.B adhoc_enabled
and role >= operator) composes a JCWF referencing pre\-deployed
scripts and submits it via
.BR run_adhoc_workflow .
The full loop:
.IP 1. 4
\fBDiscover\fR \(em
.B list_scripts
returns the catalog; the LLM picks scripts it needs.
.IP 2.
\fBCompose\fR \(em agent assembles a JCWF canvas referencing those
scripts.
.IP 3.
\fBSubmit\fR \(em
.B run_adhoc_workflow
stages the JCWF under
.B _adhoc/<user_slug>/<ts>_<counter>_del-<ts>/
and kicks off execution. Missing script references return 400
.BR missing_scripts .
.IP 4.
\fBMonitor\fR \(em
.B get_run_status
polls until terminal.
.IP 5.
\fBEnumerate\fR \(em
.B list_run_files
returns outputs with size, content\-type, SHA\-256, a local
.B path
(for same\-host agents) and a
.B download_url
(for remote agents).
.IP 6.
\fBRetrieve\fR \(em
.B get_run_file
streams an artefact. Text inline, binary base64; Range requests
supported for files over the 10 MB single\-response cap.
.IP 7.
\fBCleanup\fR \(em
.B on_completion
wipes the folder inline; TTL policies are swept by the reaper;
.B retain
needs admin action.
.PP
Every step is audit\-logged with the agent's identity, role, and (for
file reads) bytes served. Admins can read any user's run folders with an
.B admin_cross_user_read
audit trail; operators see only their own.
.
.SH AI SETUP
To use AI\-powered workflows, you need at least one API key configured.
.SS Setting up API keys
.IP 1. 4
Open the workflow editor at \fIhttp://localhost:8080/editor\fR.
.IP 2.
Navigate to the \fBSettings\fR page (gear icon) and select \fBAI Keys\fR.
.IP 3.
Add a provider name (e.g. "openai", "google", "anthropic") and paste the
corresponding API key.
.IP 4.
Click \fBSave Encrypted\fR.
You will be prompted for a master password.
This encrypts all keys into
.B keys.json.enc
in the working directory.
.SS Setting up AI models
.IP 1. 4
In the workflow editor, navigate to the \fBAI Manager\fR page.
.IP 2.
Configure one or more AI interfaces: set the API endpoint URL, model name,
parser type, and select which key provider to use.
Use \fBAPI1\fR for OpenAI and compatible providers,
\fBAPI2\fR for OpenAI Responses API (GPT\-5+),
\fBAPI3\fR for Google Gemini native,
\fBAPI4\fR for Anthropic Messages (Claude),
\fBAPI5\fR for AWS Bedrock,
or \fBAPI6\fR for Azure\-hosted OpenAI deployments.
.IP 3.
Set the default interface index or override per\-task in workflow definitions.
.PP
Supported providers include OpenAI (API1 chat completions or API2 Responses),
Google Gemini (API3 native, or API1 via the OpenAI\-compat endpoint),
Anthropic (API4 native Messages), AWS Bedrock (API5; SigV4\-signed; body
shape dispatches on \fImodelId\fR prefix), Azure OpenAI (API6; reuses API1
body shape with a deployment\-style URL and \fIapi\-key\fR header), Ollama
and any provider offering an OpenAI\-compatible chat completions API (API1).
.
.SH WORKFLOWS
JarvisAgent uses
.B JC Workflow
definition files
.RB ( .jcwf )
to describe automation pipelines as directed acyclic graphs (DAGs).
.PP
A workflow consists of:
.IP \(bu 3
\fBTasks\fR \(em individual units of work: AI calls, shell commands, Python
scripts, or internal actions.
Tasks can run in parallel when their dependencies allow it.
.IP \(bu
\fBEdges\fR \(em dependency and dataflow connections between tasks.
A task only runs after all its upstream dependencies have completed.
.IP \(bu
\fBTriggers\fR \(em how a workflow is started: manually, on a cron schedule,
or when a file appears in a watched directory.
.IP \(bu
\fBFilters\fR \(em per\-item expansion from CSV files, text line lists, or
queries.
A single task definition can fan out into many parallel instances.
.PP
Task types:
.TP
.B ai_call
Send a prompt (with optional file attachments) to an AI provider and capture
the response.
.TP
.B shell
Run a shell command or script.
Stdout and stderr are captured.
.TP
.B python
Call a Python function with structured inputs/outputs.
.TP
.B internal
Built\-in actions (e.g. file operations).
.PP
For the full specification including JSON schema, dataflow mapping, template
syntax, filter types, and per\-item expansion, see:
.PP
.RS
.B doc/JC_Workflow_Specification.md
.RE
.
.SH WORKFLOW EDITOR
The visual workflow editor is a React application served by JarvisAgent at:
.PP
.RS
\fIhttp://localhost:8080/editor\fR
.RE
.PP
Key features:
.IP \(bu 3
\fBVisual DAG editor\fR \(em drag\-and\-drop nodes, draw dependency and
dataflow edges between tasks.
.IP \(bu
\fBTask inspector\fR \(em configure task type, parameters, file inputs/outputs,
working directory, timeout, and per\-task AI interface override.
.IP \(bu
\fBWorkflow CRUD\fR \(em open, save, validate, run, and clean workflows.
.IP \(bu
\fBLive run monitoring\fR \(em task state badges update in real time via
WebSocket (running, succeeded, failed, skipped).
.IP \(bu
\fBRun controls\fR \(em start, stop, pause, and resume workflow runs.
.IP \(bu
\fBTemplate browser\fR \(em start from pre\-built workflow templates.
.IP \(bu
\fBValidation\fR \(em the backend validates the workflow structure and
reports issues with severity tiers directly on the canvas.
.IP \(bu
\fBFilter builder\fR \(em configure CSV, text_lines, or query filters
for per\-item fan\-out.
.IP \(bu
\fBLog viewer\fR \(em virtual\-scrolling log display with search and
run analysis (see
.B DASHBOARD
below for details).
.
.SH DASHBOARD
The React dashboard is served at:
.PP
.RS
\fIhttp://localhost:8080\fR
.RE
.PP
It has two tabs: a \fBDashboard\fR overview and a \fBLog Viewer\fR.
.SS Dashboard tab
.IP \(bu 3
\fBStatus bar\fR \(em live WebSocket connection indicator, active workflow
runs, AI session count, completed/failed counters, and a quit button.
.IP \(bu
\fBWorkflows panel\fR \(em lists all registered workflows with controls to
run or clean each workflow.
.IP \(bu
\fBSession managers panel\fR \(em shows active AI request sessions with
real\-time status updates.
.SS Log Viewer tab
A full\-screen, virtual\-scrolling log display (up to 100\,000 lines).
.IP \(bu 3
\fBLive streaming\fR \(em new log lines arrive in real time via WebSocket.
.IP \(bu
\fBAuto\-scroll\fR \(em follows the latest output; toggles between
\fBFollow\fR and \fBPaused\fR mode.
Click the button or scroll up to pause, click again to resume.
.IP \(bu
\fBSearch\fR \(em press \fB/\fR or \fBCtrl+F\fR to search.
Navigate matches with \fBEnter\fR (next) / \fBShift+Enter\fR (previous).
Match count and position are shown in the toolbar.
.IP \(bu
\fBColor\-coded log levels\fR \(em lines containing \fC[error]\fR are
displayed in \fBred\fR; lines containing \fC[warning]\fR or \fC[warn]\fR
are displayed in \fByellow\fR.
.IP \(bu
\fBRun Analyzer\fR \(em press \fB1\fR or click the \fBAnalyze\fR button.
An overlay panel appears showing:
.RS
.IP \(en 3
Workflow ID, run ID, state, start/end timestamps.
.IP \(en
Clickable log\-region links (start line \(em end line) to jump directly
to the run\(aqs output.
.IP \(en
\fB\(la\fR / \fB\(ra\fR arrows to cycle through previous runs
(newest first).
.IP \(en
\fBIssues list\fR \(em all warnings and errors within the run, color\-coded
by severity (red for errors, yellow for warnings).
.IP \(en
\fB\(ua\fR / \fB\(da\fR arrows to step through issues one by one; clicking
an issue jumps to its log line.
.RE
.
.SH SECURITY
.SS Studio edition
Studio has \fBno authentication\fR.
It is designed for single\-developer use on localhost.
The operator is responsible for not exposing the port beyond localhost,
reviewing AI\-generated scripts before accepting them, and keeping API keys
secure.
.SS Engine edition
Engine is designed for production deployments and includes comprehensive
security:
.IP \(bu 3
\fBMCP API key authentication.\fR
All programmatic endpoints require \fIAuthorization: Bearer mcp_...\fR.
Keys are per\-user (identity, role, adhoc flag, disk quota, retention policy)
and stored only as SHA\-256 hashes in the encrypted \fBmcp_keys.json.enc\fR.
Provisioning uses a single\-use enrollment token: admin calls
\fIPOST /api/auth/mcp\-keys/enroll\fR; user activates at
\fIPOST /api/auth/mcp\-keys/activate\fR and receives their real key \fBonce\fR.
On j9t's first run with an empty key store, a bootstrap admin enrollment
token is logged to stderr.
.IP \(bu
\fBDashboard sessions.\fR
Browser users sign in at the login page by pasting their MCP key
(\fIPOST /api/auth/login\fR) \(em the server returns an \fIHttpOnly\fR +
\fISameSite=Strict\fR cookie (plus \fISecure\fR when TLS is enabled), 8\-hour
sliding timeout. \fIPOST /api/auth/logout\fR destroys the session.
.IP \(bu
\fBKey expiry and self\-renewal.\fR
MCP keys expire 90 days after activation by default.
Users can renew themselves at \fIPOST /api/auth/mcp\-keys/self\-renew\fR without
admin help; old keys enter a 24\-hour grace period.
.IP \(bu
\fBAdhoc artefact retrieval.\fR
Files produced by adhoc runs are discoverable via
\fIGET /api/workflow\-runs/<runId>/files\fR and downloadable via
\fIGET /api/workflow\-runs/<runId>/files/<path>\fR.
Each adhoc run lives under \fB_adhoc/<user_slug>/<ts>_<counter>_del\-<ts>/\fR
so ownership is enforced by the filesystem layout as well as the handler \(em
operators see only their own runs; admins can read any run and every
cross\-user read is audit\-logged.
The download endpoint rejects path traversal, symlinks, directories, and
non\-regular files; full\-file responses carry \fBX\-Content\-SHA256\fR and are
capped at 10 MB (use \fIRange:\fR for larger files).
.IP \(bu
\fBScript catalog.\fR
\fIGET /api/scripts\fR returns the inventory of scripts under \fBscripts/\fR
that adhoc JCWFs can reference, parsed from each script's \fB@jarvis\-script\fR
metadata header.
The dashboard's \fBSettings > Scripts\fR tab displays the same catalog with a
manual \fBRefresh\fR button \(em admins who just deployed a new script can
pick it up without a j9t restart.
.IP \(bu
\fBFailed auth lockout.\fR
10 failed attempts from the same IP within 5 minutes triggers a 15\-minute
lockout (HTTP 403 with Retry\-After: 900).
.IP \(bu
\fBRBAC (Role\-Based Access Control).\fR
Three roles with descending privilege:
\fBadmin\fR \(em full access including shutdown, security logs, MCP key CRUD,
and configuration endpoints;
\fBoperator\fR \(em run control, workflow monitoring, application logs,
adhoc submission (when \fBadhoc_enabled\fR is granted on the key);
\fBviewer\fR \(em read\-only dashboard, workflow list, run status.
The role travels on the MCP key, on the session cookie derived from it, or on
the gateway\-injected \fBX\-Forwarded\-Role\fR header (default viewer when
absent).
.IP \(bu
\fBPer\-IP rate limiting.\fR
Token bucket algorithm: 100 requests/minute per IP, burst of 20.
Rate\-limited requests receive HTTP 429 with Retry\-After.
.IP \(bu
\fBHMAC\-SHA256 webhook authentication.\fR
Webhook triggers require a per\-workflow secret.
Callers must include \fIX\-Webhook\-Signature: sha256=<hex>\fR computed over
the raw request body.
In Engine mode, webhooks without a configured secret are rejected.
.IP \(bu
\fBWebSocket authentication.\fR
Browser upgrades are validated at the handshake via the session cookie
(.onaccept hook); no in\-band auth message is used.
.IP \(bu
\fBBuilt\-in TLS (HTTPS).\fR
Set \fBTlsCert\fR and \fBTlsKey\fR in config.json to serve HTTPS (default
port 8443).
If both are set but the files don\(aqt exist, j9t generates a self\-signed
localhost certificate at those paths on startup; if only one field is set, it
refuses to start.
When TLS is enabled, HSTS headers are added.
.IP \(bu
\fBGateway\-trusted identity headers.\fR
When deployed behind an API gateway (Kong, AWS API Gateway, Traefik, nginx),
configure \fBTrustedProxyHeader\fR and \fBTrustedRoleHeader\fR in config.json.
The gateway handles authentication (OIDC, MFA, SSO) and j9t reads the
authenticated user and role from the injected headers.
.IP \(bu
\fBSecurity audit logging.\fR
All auth events are logged to \fBlog/security.txt\fR (rotating, 10 MB x 5
files).
Logged events: auth success/failure, rate limit triggers, lockout triggers,
webhook accept/reject, shutdown requests, and run control actions \(em all
with IP, user identity, and timestamps.
Accessible via \fIGET /api/log/security\fR (admin role required) and the
dashboard\(aqs Security tab.
.IP \(bu
\fBSecurity response headers.\fR
All responses include Content\-Security\-Policy, X\-Frame\-Options: DENY,
X\-Content\-Type\-Options: nosniff, Referrer\-Policy, and Permissions\-Policy.
.IP \(bu
\fBRequest body size limit.\fR
Configurable via \fBMaxRequestBodyMB\fR (default 10 MB).
Oversized requests are rejected with HTTP 413 before parsing.
.PP
For the complete security model, threat analysis, and operator
responsibilities, see
.BR "doc/cyber security.md" .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B config.json
Main configuration file (must exist in the working directory).
.TP
.B keys.json.enc
Encrypted AI provider credentials (AES\-256\-GCM, master\-password protected); the only on\-disk credential store.
.TP
.B mcp_keys.json.enc
Encrypted MCP API key store (same master password as keys.json.enc;
only SHA\-256 hashes of keys are persisted).
.TP
.B _adhoc/
Per\-run folders for adhoc workflow submissions, auto\-cleaned according to
the configured retention policy.
.TP
.B workflows/
Directory containing
.B .jcwf
workflow definition files.
.TP
.B queue/
Monitored directory for incoming files.
.TP
.B log/log.txt
Application log file.
.TP
.B log/security.txt
Security audit log (Engine edition only, rotating 10 MB x 5 files).
.TP
.B integration/completions/
Shell completion scripts for bash and zsh.
.TP
.B .venv/
Python virtual environment (auto\-created by launcher scripts).
Contains markitdown.
PDF workflows additionally require
.B pandoc
(system package) and
.B mmdc
.RB ( "npm install \-g @mermaid\-js/mermaid\-cli@10.x" ).
.
